{
  "message": "The formula Q = C * V describes how much charge sits on a capacitor at a given voltage.

In this equation, Q is the stored electric charge in coulombs. C is capacitance in farads, which measures the component's physical capacity to hold charge based on its plate area, spacing, and dielectric material. V is the electric potential difference across those plates in volts.

Because charge is proportional to both capacitance and voltage, doubling the voltage across a fixed capacitor doubles the charge it holds. If you keep the voltage fixed but swap in a capacitor with twice the capacitance, it stores twice the charge.",
